{"kind": "fix", "step": 14, "ts": "2026-11-19T02:11:23.457439+00:00", "data": {"id": "wan_reload", "reason": "The outage began on the ISP side (cable modem lost ranging at 02:01:44, then failed re-registration with \"TFTP failed - configuration file NOT FOUND\" before rebooting and registering at 02:28:21); gw.lan is still routing with the pre-outage WAN lease (198.51.100.27 via 198.51.100.1, server-id 203.0.113.67) that was never renegotiated, so its WAN addressing no longer matches what the provider serves and nothing beyond the ISP gateway is reachable.", "options": {}, "outcome": "acted", "via": "report"}}
{"kind": "report", "step": 14, "ts": "2026-11-19T02:11:23.458014+00:00", "data": {"category": "wan", "host": "gw.lan", "component": "wan_dhcp_lease", "cause": "The outage began on the ISP side (cable modem lost ranging at 02:01:44, then failed re-registration with \"TFTP failed - configuration file NOT FOUND\" before rebooting and registering at 02:28:21); gw.lan is still routing with the pre-outage WAN lease (198.51.100.27 via 198.51.100.1, server-id 203.0.113.67) that was never renegotiated, so its WAN addressing no longer matches what the provider serves and nothing beyond the ISP gateway is reachable.", "evidence": ["WAN watchdog events: 02:06 decision action=none reason=modem_not_operational cm_status=NOT_SYNCHRONIZED; 02:29 reason=modem_settling; 02:30 and 02:40 entries are only \"would_reload\" (mode=observe), so the two reload decisions in this outage were never applied (cat/tail /var/db/wan-watchdog/events.jsonl)", "Modem log lines: 02:01:44 \"No Ranging Response received - T3 time-out\", 02:14:10 \"TFTP failed - configuration file NOT FOUND\", 02:28:21 \"Cable Modem Reboot because of - unknown; registration complete\" (cat /var/db/wan-watchdog/modem-events.jsonl)", "Modem now healthy from the firewall's point of view: status OPERATIONAL at 02:40 with uptime 2280s, but heavy plant errors (uncorrected 131, corrected 52070) (cat /var/db/wan-watchdog/modem-metrics.jsonl)", "state.json: {\"outage_start\":1772521560,\"reloads_in_outage\":2,\"last_action\":\"reload\",\"modem_uptime\":2280} - watchdog is in observe mode only", "gw.lan WAN interface is up and has an address: vtnet0 UP,RUNNING,LOWER_UP, inet 198.51.100.27/24, status active (ifconfig vtnet0)", "Default route still via 198.51.100.1 which resolves in ARP (00:17:10:9a:a3:96) yet drops all probes: \"3 packets transmitted, 0 packets received, 100.0% packet loss\" to 198.51.100.1 (netstat -rn; arp -a -n; ping -c 3 -n 198.51.100.1)", "Stored WAN lease predates the outage and is not due for renewal until 2026/3/5: fixed-address 198.51.100.27, option routers 198.51.100.1, dhcp-server-identifier 203.0.113.67, renew 4 2026/3/5 09:12:40 (cat /var/db/dhclient.leases.vtnet0)"], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "Reconfigure/renew the WAN interface on gw.lan (wan_reload) so it re-runs DHCP against the ISP now that the modem has re-registered and reports OPERATIONAL; then confirm the new lease/gateway actually passes traffic. Because the trigger was on the provider side (ranging T3 timeout plus a missing cable-modem config file on the ISP's TFTP server, with high uncorrected codeword counts), also have the operator open a ticket with the ISP if the link drops again after the reload. The watchdog itself needs no change - it is correctly in observe mode - but note its two reload decisions were never applied, so an operator action is what will restore service.", "detail": "Stale WAN DHCP lease after ISP-side modem resync", "fix": "wan_reload", "contributing": {"host": "external", "component": "provider_network"}}}
